! Kahu

//Kahu// — Hawaiian: guardian of a field of knowledge.
Cares for without owning. Honored for responsibility, not for power.

In the DreamNet protocol layer, ''kahu'' names ONE role at TWO scales.

* ''DreamNet scale — the corpus-kahu.'' A k-of-n quorum seated from the Amorphous Dreams Cabal
  curates the living grammar and holds kahu over the corpus bags (`lararium`, `lares`, the genesis
  artifact). The Cabal names the community; the kahu seat names the keys and the threshold it seats.
* ''Nexus scale — the kahu quorum.'' Each Nexus seats its own k-of-n kahu quorum, which
  holds ''admit, kapae and seal'' for that Nexus and nothing beyond it (#/nexus-scale).

At DreamNet scale the role differs from every other capability level in one way: the
corpus-kahu CURATE a grammar they never legislate. The grammar holds no versions; it lives
and drifts by use, like a natural language. The corpus-kahu lift lived constructions into
the formal register (`sigil-*.tid`) as lexicographers, and nothing ratifies a lift. The
grammar enforces itself — through register collapse detection, the ABILITY_LADDER, and each
vessel's own founding ceremony. No living authority tells a vessel what to do. The treaty does.

<<~ ahu #/contract>>

!! The Treaty Model

The corpus bags hold the written record of DreamNet's grammar:

* `lararium` — the engine and protocol package definitions
* `lares` — the API grammar and canonical meme vocabulary
* genesis artifact — the founding CRDT document that bootstraps every vessel

These bags do not contain user content, operator wikis, or social state. They carry
''structure'': types, laws, sigils, procedure tiddlers, architectural invariants.

A kahu guardian holds `cap=admin` on these bags specifically. That capability rung matches
the one any operator could receive on their own vessel's bags — //which bag// carries the grant
draws the distinction, never a new rung (`CapabilityAccess` reads `"read" | "admin"`, and no verb ladder stands).

''Use leads, and the record follows.'' Hearths speak the grammar, and what use entrenches the
corpus-kahu lift into the corpus bags as curation. A vessel that syncs those bags inherits the
record via CRDT propagation — never via a command from the corpus-kahu. Speakers lead; the kahu
record; the protocol distributes; the vessels enforce
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/living-grammar-palace#/three-registers">>).

''Intelligibility between hearths runs GRADED, like dialects.'' Graceful parsing and the
quoteblock floor carry whatever a reader does not know, so a construction one hearth lacks
degrades to legible text rather than refusing
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/graceful-parsing">>).

!!! The engine cadence reads as the highest-risk lever in the whole design

''AN ENGINE CADENCE STANDS AS THE ONE VERSIONED INSTRUMENT ANY HAND HOLDS, and the record
shows it stranding federations rather than governing them.'' The worked case runs exact:
a founding node rewrote its own protocol, the network it had convened did not follow,
and a fork formed to keep the abandoned grammar alive.
''No malice, no capture, no command — an authoring act, propagated as designed, that
emptied the federation it propagated across.'' The same author later moved the successor
grammar into a standards body, and ''that grammar outlived every implementation he
wrote'' — which names the cure as ''move the load-bearing artifact somewhere it survives
its author'', never as authoring more carefully.

''AND A LIVING GRAMMAR MAKES DRIFT INVISIBLE UNTIL IT HAS HAPPENED.'' Grammar drift
separates no Nexus — the engine window binds membership — so two groups of hearths drift apart in
use, each still reading itself fluently, ''each still computing itself one Nexus'', and
nothing anywhere reports how far their dialects stand apart. The absent roster — a shelter property everywhere else — costs
exactly this: ''no instrument surfaces a fork.'' A lag distribution across the carriage
board reads as the nearest available proxy and nobody has built it.

''What the engine cadence therefore owes'': an engine epoch that a vessel MAY decline while
staying reachable and syncing — the partial-conformance seat the identity plane already
names (//not a member, not an exile, a member with reduced sovereignty//) — so the
cadence bends rather than strands. ''Forkability protects against a drifting cadence; it
protects nothing against a cadence shipping correctly to a mesh that cannot keep up.''

<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/identity-enacts-relation#/the-gauge">>

!!! The engine cadence stands its own joint ~ never carriage, never the kahu quorum

''THE ENGINE BINDS MEMBERSHIP, through a rolling WINDOW.'' TW5 core plus the house code, by CID,
names a hearth's true-name (`hearthTrueName` = `engineCid`), and every device-delegation edge signs
it. A Nexus admits a rolling window of engine epochs, never one engine CID, so its hearths need not
all run one engine. ''A Nexus's engine cadence comes OFFERED by a cadence threshold seated apart from
its kahu quorum'', held by those who bear the reboot cost (operators whose places run the engine), and
that threshold advances the window (the cadence lineage, below). ''Each device moves only by its own adoption'' — a reboot onto the offered
engine — and no act moves it for it. ''Carrying never counts'': a lamplighter runs infrastructure by
contract, carries sealed bytes it cannot read, presents and never condemns, and carrying confers no
share in any Nexus's cadence.

''The grammar holds no ratchet.'' `grammarCid`, the grammar epoch and `kahuli grammar` stand marked
to RETIRE in code; a scout's measurement of their consumers gates that cut
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/epoch-binding-surfaces#/the-five">>).

''The cadence never fuses with the kahu quorum.'' The hands that admit, kapae and seal for a
Nexus (#/nexus-scale) roll no engine by holding that seat, and a cadence act never moves the admission
price — two joints, two thresholds, two rates (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/six-joints">>).
''And it runs PER-NEXUS: the NEXUS fences, never the cabal'' — an epoch means nothing outside the island
that rolls it.

''The window reads as a FRONTIER over a signed offer lineage.'' The cadence threshold signs offer
events, each linked by `prev` to the one before; an engine stands while its offer sits within k hops
of the head a hearth holds and no release-deny names it. ''k rides each offer'', owned by the cadence
joint, never by canon or the charter. The window advances and ages only by offers — never by time,
never by counting who runs what — and a release-deny drops an engine mid-window, monotone. Below the
floor an edge lapses by non-renewal, and the device re-mints once it adopts. ''A Nexus offers only
engines the corpus-kahu lineage contains'': Nexuses choose WHEN, never WHAT.

!!! The cadence lineage ~ a seated threshold that governs itself

''THE CADENCE THRESHOLD STANDS AS A SELF-GOVERNING LINEAGE, seated over the operator relation.'' It
names a threshold that operators hold, never a fourth base relation beside walking, contracting and
seating (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-three-admissions">>). The
lineage carries its own inception, and a Nexus's joint founding cites that inception ONCE, seating the first
cadence set. A household seed carries no cadence inception: it serves its one holder alone, and that holder's own hand bears its reboots. From there the lineage governs itself — its own per-seat pre-rotation (#/nexus-scale), and
its own threshold-signed seat events that seat and unseat cadence holders.

''ELIGIBILITY READS AS A PRESENTATION, never a roster.'' A candidate presents a counted operator admit
for this Nexus AID together with a device edge whose engine sits in the window — the two facts that
make the reboot cost theirs to bear. Nothing lists who stands eligible; each candidacy proves itself.

''THE TWO THRESHOLDS NEVER REACH INTO EACH OTHER.'' The kahu quorum never offers an engine, and the
cadence threshold never admits anyone. A kahu revoke of an operator only SURFACES that operator's
cadence seat for the cadence quorum's review — the road-(iv) shape (#/nexus-scale): the seat stands
until the cadence quorum unseats it openly by its own seat event, and no revoke cascades across the joint.

''Two letters, two counts'': ''t'' names the signing threshold (how many cadence seats sign an offer, KERI's `nt`), and
''k'' names the window (how many hops an offer may trail the head). ''The clan default seats t = 1'': in a small
clan one operator offers the engine, and t grows as more places bear the cost. The lineage signs the `prev`-linked engine offers the
window above reads, and each device adopts at reboot.

''EXIT RUNS BY FORK.'' Any hearth may stop following the lineage, and no act of the lineage reaches a
hearth that declined it.

''The joint founding cites the cadence inception once.'' The cadence inception image carries the first cadence
set's keys, per-seat next-key digests and threshold. Its digest names the image's canonical bytes. A joint
charter's genesis carries `founding: { offer, cadence }`; `cadence` cites that digest, and the charter AID
commits the citation. The cadence image names no Nexus AID. Every holder signs the image; the charter reader
binds those holders to the founding seats and checks each signature. A household seed carries `founding: null`
and no cadence inception.

''What the code holds'': `cadence-lineage.ts` signs and verifies the cadence inception. The joint founding cites one inception signed by both hearths; each hearth keeps its own `cadence-key` row. ''Owed in code'': cadence seat events and the offer verifier. The household seed cites none; its one holder chooses each reboot.

''Still open'', each unruled:

* whether one daemon may host islands at two engine epochs;
* whether partial conformance must land before `kahuli engine` un-holds.

<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/lamplighters#/welded">>

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/seed-and-nexus>>

!! Seed and Nexus ~ one charter shape, two readings

''ONE CHARTER SHAPE CARRIES TWO READINGS'' (operator-ruled). A Lararium that one operator founds stands as
a ''SEED'': a household island with full machinery — its own charter, herm, leaves and hosted guests. A
''NEXUS'' stands when two operators each seat their own key in one charter. A ''seat'' names one chair in
a charter's k-of-n; a seat holds a key, never a person. ''The phase turns on a second operator's SEAT,
never on a carriage contract'': a contract admits a carrier and seats nobody. A Nexus names the
relationship between operators (quote license: //"a 'nexus' describes the operators relationship, which
flows down through their infra's relationships with each other's nodes and leafs and herms/many-to-many"//).

''The ladder'': a seed → a Nexus of two seats → an enduring Nexus of three or more.

''A SEED FOUNDS IN ONE ACT, AND ITS AID NEVER MOVES.'' The Stable founding act (`lares vessel found --hearth`)
yields, under one human route, the place, persona zero, and a PRIVATE seed charter of one seat at threshold one:
the seat's own key, and its cold next key on one card the person reads back before the charter moves. Persona
zero's face founds after the charter, so its fleet relations bind the seed charter's AID, the hearth's true name:
its device edge, its dyad scope and its cabal sentinel's seed. A seed grows its chairs by the roll that card
arms, and a re-seat over a seed charter a face binds refuses by name, since a second genesis would mint another
AID and orphan every relation bound to the first. A face founds at a hearth alone: on a place with no seed
charter, `persona new 0` refuses and names the act.

''A NEXUS FOUNDS FRESH.'' Two founders make a fresh joint genesis beside each household's own seed, at
threshold two of two. Nobody grows a household's own charter into a Nexus: a second operator's key seated
there hands that partner quorum over the household. A household seed never takes a foreign seat
(`foreignSeats`, `packages/lares-cli/src/seal-import.ts`, refuses it by fingerprint).

''TWO VAULTS, CHECKED IN PERSON.'' The co-founding act takes one seat key from each founder's own vault.
A seat's ''fingerprint'' names a short digest of its key, and each founder reads the other's fingerprint
aloud before either signs. The ceremony claims no proof of two humans: two keys never prove two humans,
and the house accepts that cost
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/cryptographic-individual">>).

''A SEAT HOLDS ITS OWN KEY.'' A seat key stands apart from the persona root and apart from the dialing
leaf. Each seat commits the digest of its own ''cold key'': the next key, kept on paper off the machine
and used only to roll. The shared reserve seed and its three cards stand retired: the quorum rite prints
none and arms no head that cannot roll (#/nexus-scale).

''LOSS AT ALPHA.'' A lost cold key at a two-seat Nexus ends that Nexus: the founders found a successor and
re-issue its admits. Recovery contacts recover a PERSON, never a Nexus seat. A seed that loses both its node and
its paper gets re-founded.

''THE ENDURING SHAPE.'' Two seats FOUND a Nexus. A Nexus meant to endure SHOULD seat a third chair early
and pass chairs by its own rolls before loss forces a successor. That intent adds no mechanism beyond
Kahu admission by roll.

''A SEAT NEEDS NO PUBLIC HANDLE.'' A seat carries a private chair label and a fingerprint. The exported
charter publishes each seat's key and fingerprint, and no chair's Handle, pet-name or label
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/two-layer-petnames">>).

''FOUNDERS HOLD BOTH STACKS AS TWO SIGNED EVENTS EACH'': a seat in the genesis (the Kahu stack), and a
carriage contract that the subject signs and the seats countersign (the Lamplighter stack). A founder may
countersign their own contract; the other seat signs too. That yields two signatures, never two
independent reviews.

''ADMISSION TAKES THE SEAT QUORUM'' (operator-ruled; quote license: //"Pono, quorum for Kahu and
Lamplighter invites, another limit on 'user' friend invites sounds pono."//). Admitting a Kahu — a roll
that adds a seat — and admitting a Lamplighter — a countersigned carriage contract for an operator or a
place — each take the charter's seat quorum. No single-seat class covers either. Kapae and rolls take the
quorum too. At two seats the quorum means both. A quorum's act over a vouch or a member edge takes the same
quorum, and a voucher's own withdrawal takes the voucher's one signature
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/edge-hands">>).

''THE USER ROAD STANDS SEPARATE.'' A hosted friend's leaf under an operator's own household takes no Kahu
quorum and carries its own bound: a user introduces, an operator admits, and the answer consumes the
introduction. What the code holds: a clockless allowance of `min(1 + rolls survived, cap)` per hosting
epoch (`lares host roll --cap`); a host-minted guest at floor 1; a walker-minted guest vesting at the
host's next roll; single-use blind tokens burned at the host; revocation by a roll or by a kapae of the
guest's leaf; no bound on the operator's own `lares host invite`. ''Owed in code'': a walker's invite
earns standing by a roll alone, with no operator answer
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite">>). The bound rides three
dials on the hosting act, each a count and none a clock: open introductions per guest, a pre-answered
allowance for a trusted guest, and strangers' introductions held. An introduction the operator answers
arrives signed; a pre-answered allowance stays blind; the operator's own invite carries no bound.

''A FACE CLAIMS ITS OWN HANDLE; THE NEXUS ANSWERS FOR ITS OWN TABLE.'' A Handle binds by the face's own
announce and takes no quorum and no permission: a gate on the claim would stand as a registry
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/two-layer-petnames">>). A Nexus's public DNS
domain presents the Handles seated at that Nexus through the Nexus's own steward-signed answer; that
answer speaks for the Nexus's table and grants no name. The quorum removes, by kapae, and never names.
Whoever holds the domain's DNS credential decides what the zone says, so the domain acts as its
keeper's hand, never as a consensus.

''RECOVERY CONTACTS STAY OPTIONAL AND PER FACE.'' Every face starts one-of-one self. No recovery contact key ever serves
two faces, and a recovery contact set recovers a face's key lineage, never its memory
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/distinct-recovery">>).

!!! RULED for the first ceremony (operator ruling)

Each line reads whole at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings">>.

* ''At two seats no seat signs any Kahu act alone.''
* ''An operator's own herm'' takes one signature, scoped to one place and one Nexus, and falls with a kapae of the operator's own admit.
* ''A seat gets ADDED, never replaced'': the standing Kahu add a holder to both cabals, and the founders keep their chairs.
* ''Paper'': the cold-key card takes the house format — 64 hex plus a checksum, in groups of four — and prints its purpose, its chair label, its generation and a short charter fingerprint.
* ''No recovery contacts on the day'': each face starts one-of-one on its own paper, and enrolment comes later by one typed event on the face's own log, signed by the face's own key (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/persona-kel#/the-enrolment">>).
* ''A per-Nexus key mints at random'', and the face's lineage re-issues one by a signed hand-over the gate verifies (#/nexus-scale).

!!! OPEN ~ what the operator owns

Each item below stands unruled. Nothing in code decides one.

* ''Recovery contact request authentication.'' Options: an agreed channel and a live voice · in person · a device-derived event plus a short string, named a byte check and never an identity proof.
* ''The veto.'' Options: keep it as a house fork rule and specify it · drop it.
* ''Death and succession of a person.'' Options: recovery contact recovery under the same lineage · a separate succession object · no path.
* ''The backup bundle.'' What it holds and which verb imports it. A bundle per face keeps faces unlinked on the backup medium.
* ''The catastrophe rite and any third key.'' Options: no rite · an opt-in, charter-native seat-recovery rite with named witnesses · an outsider's third cold key. At alpha no rite stands, and LOSS AT ALPHA governs.

!!! The surface ~ where each part reads

<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings">> the founding acts, the fence and the build order
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-three-admissions">> walking, contracting and seating, and which take the quorum
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/identity-classes#/promotion-path">> the class ladder and how an operator takes a seat
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/distinct-recovery">> recovery contacts per face, and lineage apart from memory
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/two-layer-petnames">> Handles, pet-names and the private chair label
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/lamplighters#/office">> the Lamplighter office and its quorum grant
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/second-operator">> the household walk for a seed, then a Nexus
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-key-hierarchy">> the keys a face presents at a gate
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-wax-stamp">> the charter's epoch chain as provenance

''What the code holds.'' `nexus seal show` reads SEED or NEXUS from two counts — seated keys this vault can
produce, and seated keys it cannot (`nexus-phase.ts`) — and claims no proof of two humans; a
seat claim takes no Handle and carries a private chair label; `nexus seal export` carries fingerprints and
no label. A chair's key rests as a sealed seat row on the vessel whose face stood for it, minted at random
apart from every persona root (`seat-key-store.ts`, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table">>):
`seal seat` mints it, prints the chair's NEXT key as a cold-key card the operator reads back before the charter
moves, and commits one digest per chair (`nextKeyDigests`); `seal rotate` takes each card back on a terminal and
adopts each revealed key as its chair's seat key; the quorum doors sign with the rows (`held-quorum.ts`). An
earlier charter shape (one `nextKeyCommit` per epoch) reads by name and licenses nothing. `seal cold rehearse`
reads a card back against the head's digests, names the chair it would roll, and moves nothing; `--cold-key`
stands in for its terminal only on a staged root whose custody proves the published test route. The Stable founding
act stands in code (`found-hearth.ts`, `hearth-aid.ts`): one seat, its card, the PRIVATE seed charter, persona zero's
face binding its AID; `seal-ceremony.ts` refuses a re-seat over it (`hearth-bound`). `seal cofound open|join|seal`
founds a joint charter with one key from each vault and one cadence inception signed by both hearths. The two-root
witness (`tests/e2e/nexus-two-root.test.ts`) founds, contracts, rolls and restarts two staged hearths with files as
the only courier, and refuses an unsigned or half-signed charter at both imports.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/nexus-lifecycle>>

!! The Nexus lifecycle ~ born by act, lives by traffic, quiet by absence

A Nexus names vessels syncing across ley lines. Liveness reads off the traffic itself; nobody declares or grants it. Nobody dissolves a Nexus; it goes quiet. A cabal realm cools to anu without deletion (`cabal-realm`); a standing lift expires without a lowering act (`lararium-canonical-model`). ''The correct admin capability here reads as NONE.''

<<~ has Stage Birth "verb/nexus-seal-seat admin/none ~ a founding act seats keys in a genesis charter">>
<<~ has Stage Advance "verb/nexus-seal-rotate admin/none ~ revealed key-set matches the prior pre-commitment">>
<<~ has Stage Succession "verb/nexus-seal-rotate admin/none ~ the roster becomes exactly what stands">>
<<~ has Stage Liveness "verb/none admin/none ~ ley-line traffic; nothing to build">>
<<~ has Stage Quiet "verb/none admin/none ~ absence of traffic; no dissolution act">>

Succession rides the roll law at #/seed-and-nexus and #/nexus-scale. A Kahu takes a chair by a quorum roll that seats a key; a seat needs no Handle. The reveal must match the prior epoch's pre-commitment, so no hand adds or drops a Kahu quietly. ''What the code holds'': `seal rotate` takes each revealed chair's card back on a terminal and adopts its key as that chair's seat key (`packages/lares-cli/src/commands/nexus-seal.ts`); a hearth's seed charter founds in the Stable founding act and grows only by that roll, since a re-seat over it refuses (`hearth-bound`). `seal cofound open|join|seal` founds a two-of-two charter across two vaults. `seal roll open|join|sign|file` rolls a charter whose seats rest in several vaults as one carried circle: each holder contributes its own chair and fresh successor, types the rule and every other chair's fingerprint as heard, and signs one image (`packages/lararium-node/src/roll-circle-ceremony.ts`); a new holder joins with a key the head never committed and signs it (possession), a later roll passes a chair, and a vault that sat a roll out catches up by importing the newest charter along the one lineage. The roll's anchor follows the filed head (`seal anchor`).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/nexus-scale>>

!! The Nexus scale ~ a kahu quorum holds admit, kapae and seal

''FOUNDING READS AS AN ACT, NEVER A FACE.'' The founding mints the Nexus's name — the genesis AID
commits the signed inception image, whose seats, rule, successor commitments and joint offer fingerprint name one founding — and seats the first kahu quorum. Every seated key signs that image. The founder then holds a
''kahu seat in THAT Nexus'', and nothing more: no founder face and no `found` cap outlives the act.
`vessel found` stays a rite verb every operator runs (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/found">>).

''The quorum holds three acts for its one Nexus'': ''admit'' (counter-signing an operator's or a
place's contract-in), ''kapae'' (the quorum-signed antigen, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/carry-contract#/kapae-the-antigen">>)
and ''seal'' (the k-of-n roll of the seal lineage, which seats and unseats the quorum's own members). A
Kahu face therefore carries one cap — the Nexus-scale `quorum-share`. The engine cadence rides a separate
threshold (#/contract), and the corpus-kahu curate at DreamNet scale.

''An admit names ONE relation'': an operator or a place CONTRACTING into the Nexus mesh. A user never
holds one — a user walks on an invite a hosting hearth or a walker it hosts hands them, redeemed at the
host's own gate for a keyed grant — and a kahu seat never rides one — a
member takes a seat through the seal lineage. Admitting a Lamplighter and seating a Kahu each take
the seat quorum (#/seed-and-nexus). The three stay apart
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-three-admissions">>).

!!! ★ THE SUBJECT PRESENTS ITS ADMIT ★

''The admit travels WITH its subject.'' At the wire the subject PRESENTS its quorum-signed admit, and
the carriage gate checks it against the shared ''DENY-only'' board — the kapae antigen, monotone, a
set that only tightens. ''No folded allow roster gates carriage, at any scope'': a roster of who
belongs names the one register the laws forbid (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/registry-filter">>).
The quorum's admits stay a shared contract RECORD; the gate never folds that record into an allow set.

''What the code holds.'' `verifyPresentedAdmit` (`carriage-registry.ts`) reads a presented admit and
its causal lineage against the deny-only board and the kapae antigen; it folds no allow roster and
reads no clock. `packages/lararium-mesh/src/leaf-presentation.ts` carries the presentation; the gate keeps it per socket as untrusted input, deciding nothing (`every arm grants nothing on arrival`), and the sealed face gate returns a verdict before `GateSessionChannel` opens. A vessel
carries a SET of Nexuses (`carried-set.ts`). No relation witness and no signing domain of its own
stand beside the gate: an admit proves itself by its quorum signatures and its lineage.

''The daemon's carriage decision rides the verifier.'' `nexus-carriage.ts` seats a peer in its leaf map
only when the socket that peer stands on PRESENTED an admit and `verifyPresentedAdmit` reads it `held`
against a Nexus this vessel carries; a peer presenting nothing stays a stranger, whatever the board
records. `foldCarriageSet` reads the board's RECORD (`nexus members --list`) and no enforcement shore
reads its answer as an allow set.

''The subject stamp keys on the Nexus scope.'' An admit signed by a PersonaGroup ROOT correlates one
human across every Nexus that admits them, so the subject stamp signs with the face's own
key at that Nexus: 32 bytes minted at random where the face first presents there, resting sealed and cold in
the face's row for that Nexus (`nexus-leaf.ts`), and related by no seed to the root or to the face's key at any
other Nexus. ''The face's lineage re-issues that key'' by a hand-over the standing key and its successor both
sign (`lares persona reissue`). The record rides the Nexus's carriage board; the admit keeps naming the key the
Nexus admitted; a gate stands a presenter under the HEAD of that key's line alone and reads a line that forks
as denied; and a kapae on any key of the line holds the whole line. A compromise of the key takes the face-key
roll (a revoke and a fresh admit), never a hand-over. ''A seat holds its own key'', apart from the persona root
and apart from the dialing key (#/seed-and-nexus), and the quorum's chairs sign with those seat keys.

''The gate's shape, ruled'':

* a presentation carries its lineage, and the admit travels as a carried bundle;
* a dialer presents only the dialed island's admit;
* a seated chair implies its Nexus stands in the carried set;
* an unsettled deny refuses;
* a reader learns a rotation only by re-import.

!!! The seal lineage rolls by per-seat pre-rotation

''EACH SEAT PRE-ROTATES ITS OWN KEY.'' The charter commits per seat: a list of next-key digests and a
next threshold (`nextKeyDigests[]` · `nextThreshold`, KERI's `n[]` · `nt`), never one digest over a
shared key-set. `seal seat` mints each chair's COLD next key, prints it as a cold-key card the operator
reads back before the charter moves, and commits its digest; the next key rests on its card, never on a vessel.
A routine roll takes k per-seat REVEALS. ''A card opens the one generation it prints'' (house reading under the
cold-card law), so a digest left unrevealed carries forward nowhere: every roll lists fresh digests alone, and a
chair whose card stays unrevealed leaves at that roll. It returns only as an added seat, whose key signs the roll
as its proof of possession. ''No recovery contact takes part in a routine roll.''

''A KEY THE LINEAGE SEATED NEVER SEATS AGAIN, the genesis's own among them.'' A rolled epoch names its seats in its
roll; the genesis inception image names its seats and threshold, and every seat signs that image. The FIRST roll reveals those seats and their
threshold again, which must hash to the genesis's key-set (`roll-genesis-unrevealed`, `roll-genesis-unbound`), and no later
roll carries them. Every roll then refuses a key any earlier epoch seated (`roll-reuses-a-seated-key`).

''Built'': the charter commits `nextKeyDigests[]` · `nextThreshold` per seat (`wax-stamp.ts`), a roll takes
each card back on a terminal (`seal rotate`), `seal cold rehearse` reads a card back and moves nothing, and an
earlier shape (one `nextKeyCommit` per epoch) reads by name and licenses nothing, and a roll that lists any
digest an earlier epoch listed refuses (`roll-respends-a-digest`). The signed roll below stands in code.

''THE KEYS A ROLL REVEALS SIGN IT.'' At least threshold of the revealed keys — the keys whose
digests the prior epoch committed, which carry that epoch's authority — sign the roll over the prior
epoch's CID, the revealed key-set, the threshold and the next commitment, under the `seal-roll` domain. A
key a seat change adds, committed by no prior digest, signs the same bytes as its proof of possession.
The lineage verifier checks those signatures at every step, so revealed PUBLIC keys forge nothing: an
epoch stands only where the private halves of the committed keys signed it
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-wax-stamp">>).

''THE COLD MEDIUM'': paper words carrying a checksum, read back as a proof before the digest seals; a
hardware key stays optional. Never a passphrase-sealed file on the vessel, and never a key derived from
the persona seed — a cold key that shares a breach with the warm one buys nothing.

''FROST WITHIN A SEAT, MULTISIG ACROSS SEATS.'' One seat may spread its key across its holder's devices
by threshold signing. Seats sign as distinct keys, because a quorum change must see WHICH seat signed.

!!! The recovery contact stands a face apart ~ Recovery contacts recover the PERSON

''RECOVERY CONTACTS RECOVER THE PERSON, never the Nexus.'' A Recovery contact holds a recovery key over a persona's KEL
and, at recovery, signs that persona's rotation independently; nothing reconstructs
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/threshold-attest-recovery">>). When a seat
holder loses their keys, their recovery contacts rotate the person back into continuity. Where a quorum survives
(three or more seats), that quorum re-seats the person by its own roll (`seal reseat`). At two seats no
quorum survives a lost cold key, and that Nexus ends (#/seed-and-nexus). ''Recovery contacts carry no weight in the
Nexus KEL.'' A recovery contact cuts a fresh key for each face they guard
(<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/persona-policy#/distinct-recovery">>). ''Owed in code — the
re-found'': `seal reseat` rides the per-seat commitment and stands unbuilt.

''ROAD (iv): A QUORUM CHANGE SURFACES, it never cascades.'' A seat change re-seats nothing on its own.
The house derives, from records already on the board, which admits and offices leaned on a departed
signer, and surfaces that set to the new quorum, which revokes openly, by name, on the deny board.
Standing carries until then. The set derives from the board's own records, so no roster forms.

''THE CATASTROPHE RITE'' stands OPEN and unbuilt (#/seed-and-nexus). At alpha a seed re-founds and a
two-seat Nexus founds a successor. A rite, once ruled, would name its own weights and witnesses.

''One human MAY hold both faces.'' The separation lives in the OBJECTS, never in the person: a recovery
key and a quorum-share stay two keys in two custodies, so one stolen object never opens both. A SEED
begins with one operator holding its seat and its recovery keys; a Nexus stands when two operators each
seat their own key (#/seed-and-nexus).

''Retired'': the shared reserve seed and the reserve-reveal verb.

''Built — the re-found's seat half.'' The per-seat commitment and signed inception image move charter bytes, epoch CIDs and the Nexus AID. The charter carries one digest per seat; the rite prints each chair's cold-key card and
`seal rotate` takes the cards back; the Stable founding act founds a hearth's seed charter at one seat, threshold
one, under its card (`found-hearth.ts`). ''Owed in code'': `seal reseat` and the catastrophe rite. The field's principles for a recovery
object and its reader rest on the shelf (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pattern-integrities#/shelf">>).

!!! Owed in code ~ names that blur the relations

* ''The vessel key signs for two relations'' — the place's carrier seal and the wire's proof (the gate
  key every Nexus a hearth carries shares) — where each relation wants its own face. No invite
  rides it: the hearth's per-Nexus leaf signs its hosting act. The per-Nexus wire key rides the wire's socket cutover, where every vessel-key gate proof leaves and the floor
  announcement signs under the wire key.

The cabal ⊥ quorum split stands in code where it can:

* ''`lares nexus rite quorum`'' seats the kahu QUORUM: it gives each chair that stands a seat key, seats the
  genesis epoch, prints each chair's cold-key card, arms the genesis with one digest per chair, and mints no
  person.
* ''`KahuQuorumSeats`'' names the quorum's seats — a seated key-set, its threshold and the charter epoch
  it authorizes. Keys, never people.
* ''`sentinel/kahu-cabal`'' (`MESH_CABAL_SENTINEL_URI`) names the CABAL: a Keyhive membership document
  whose members stand as PersonaGroups, with no threshold, AID or succession. A face founded at a hearth seeds it
  under that hearth's seed-charter AID (`meshCabalSentinelUriFor`), so two hearths' cabals seed apart. Its digest
  seeds the document's Keyhive ChangeId and rides Keyhive's signed events, so it renames only by a deliberate re-key.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/chartered-classes>>

!! Chartered classes ~ the charter's own seats set every class, and a promotion approves exact bytes

''THE CLASS TABLE READS OFF THE CHARTER EPOCH, AND NO CHARTER BYTE MOVES.'' With n distinct seated keys and
the charter's threshold, the table derives (`charteredClassTable`, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/chartered-classes.ts`):

* ''One seat'': every class takes that one seat.
* ''Two seats'': every class takes both. At two seats no seat signs any Kahu act alone.
* ''Three seats or more'': ''ordinary'' takes one; ''high-impact'' takes the larger of the charter's threshold
  and a strict majority, capped at n; ''emergency'' takes two and leaves a review owed.

A ''revocation'' takes the threshold of the act it revokes. The high-impact surfaces stand `charter`,
`admission`, `trust-root` and `floor-release` (`HIGH_IMPACT_SURFACES`, the one list), read through one fold (trim, lower case, a run of space or underscore reads as a
hyphen), so `Charter` and `TRUST_ROOT` never fall to the ordinary class.

''A PROMOTION APPROVES EXACT BYTES, NEVER A NAME.'' The approval (`promotion-approval.ts`, domain
`promotion-approval`) signs the bytes' `ni:///sha-256;` hash together with the purpose, the surface, the class
it claims and the charter epoch it roots on. A bare CID is no approval; other bytes under the same name read
`bytes-differ`; a class below its surface's class, another epoch, or a count short of the class threshold each
refuse. ''No promotion rides the emergency power''. A seat signs only a statement a person confirms by its digest (the bytes hash, purpose, surface, class and epoch together), and the
seats on two vessels each sign a carried partial (`lares nexus promote open|sign|check`). The approval governs a
crossing into `@crossroads`: `lares meme promote --dest-bag lar:///ha.ka.ba/bags/crossroads --approval <file>`
refuses before anything moves unless the current charter's seats approved those exact bytes, and the receipt names
the approval's statement digest. A crossing into a vessel's own canon asks no charter.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/cabal-admission>>

!! Cabal admission ~ the seats admit a PersonaGroup into the Kahu cabal; a vouch admits nobody

''ADMISSION STANDS ITS OWN AUTHORITY ACT.'' A PersonaGroup enters a Nexus's Kahu cabal by an act the charter's
seats sign at the high-impact threshold (`kahu-cabal-admission.ts`, domain `kahu-cabal-admit`). A removal
counts at the admission's own threshold. The door cites the group's standing heads on every act it opens; a
removal that cites no head stands concurrent with the admission, and concurrent admit and remove heads read
''unsettled'', never held. A vouch, an invite, or any record of another kind reads `not-an-admission`.

''THE ACT RESTS ON THE CARRIAGE BOARD'' every member already carries, under its own prefix
(`$:/lar/kahu-cabal/`); the carriage reader skips it by kind. The act writes no Keyhive membership: the
sentinel add (`sentinel/kahu-cabal`, #/nexus-scale) stays its own capability act. A removal therefore closes the access
path for every reader that consults it, while the group's sentinel capability stands until its own removal.

''A ROLL CLOSES THE EPOCH AN ACT COUNTED UNDER.'' An act counts only under the charter epoch it names, so after
a seal roll every admission stops counting (`wrong-charter-epoch`, act by act), the group's standing reads
''absent'', and the seats re-admit at the new head: that act cites no head and carries no lineage back to the
closed epoch. ''Owed in
code'': carrying cabal acts across a roll by anchored ancestry, as the carriage fold carries a closed-epoch
admit (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/carry-contract">>).

''THE ACCESS PATH TO SHARED CANON READS BOTH FACTS'' (`kahuCabalCanonAccess`): it holds only for a group the
seats admitted AND the sentinel holds. Admitted alone, or joined alone, refuses. The seats may rest on two
vessels: one opens and signs a partial, the other confirms its CID, adds its seat and lands it
(`lares nexus cabal admit|remove|cosign|standing`).

''THE FOUNDING LANDS THE FIRST ADMISSION.'' A one-seat founding signs a counted 1-of-1 act at its one seat that admits
the group holding the hearth's Kahu realm (`found-hearth.ts`, `landFoundingAdmission`); a founding run again lands no
second act. `kahuRealmGroup` (`kahu-canon-reach.ts`) names that group through ONE accessor: persona zero's group
today, and Wave C's Kahu-face group swaps in at that function alone, since the realm's storage never keys on the group.

''ONE PREDICATE GUARDS EVERY WRITE DOOR.'' `requireKahuReach` (`kahu-canon-reach.ts`) calls `kahuCabalCanonAccess` for
the hand at the door, before any write: the residency reactor with every verb it carries (a MOVE out of canon too,
since it tombstones the source), `meme put` and `meme delete`, draft placement, and a realm steward's write, whose
realm grant stands in for no check. The shore hands the island the charter's roster and the board's acts
(`readKahuCabalBoard`); the island adds the sentinel read and the group at ACCESS, never at boot
(`kahu-reach-source.ts`). A vessel that composes no source reads as a stranger and refuses a system bag. A READ and a
copy OUT of canon ask nothing.

''THE LIVE SYSTEM DOCUMENTS REST IN THE REALM.'' The founding mints one document per system bag and registers each as a
realm bag of the hearth's realm, sealed by persona zero's per-Nexus key as the steward (`kahu-realm.ts`,
`landKahuRealm`; tier `contract`, kept by that one name). A document's id derives from the steward's own seed, a MAC under persona zero's per-Nexus seed over the Nexus and the bag (`kahuBagDocUrl`), so no peer can pre-seed the canon a founding adopts and a founding retried under that leaf registers the same document. A reader never recomputes it: the Kahu canon counts only registrations sealed by exactly that steward, the hearth keeps each registration it sealed and the kept copy stands first where it verifies, and the steward's seals over two documents for one bag stand none. What stays owed (roadmap row `P26`): a member's synced change counts only under Kahu reach, the steward's succession across a leaf reissue and a paper restore, and a whole-record conflict resolution verb. A vessel NAMES them at boot only where the board carries a
counted admission for the Kahu realm's group (`standKahuCanon`): the registrations come off the realm doc folded and
seal-verified, the keel mounts exactly what it names (`keel.systemCanon`), and the registered bag set, the `bags/lares`
and `bags/lararium` disk mirrors and the system wikis' document urls follow the same naming. A vessel outside the cabal
holds no pointer and no layer for either bag, projects nothing under `bags/`, and reads the floor through `@oracle`. No
catalog row names a system bag. A browser composes no board reader, so it reads as a stranger and keeps the floor; a
browser Kahu hand rides Wave C's face context.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/floor-release>>

!! The floor release ~ the Nexus's one public answer

''ONE PORCH BODY PER NEXUS.'' The Kahu cabal releases a floor by one body, `{kind, nexus, epoch, prior, manifest}`: the
`floor-release` signing domain as `kind`, the Nexus AID, the charter epoch whose seats sign, the CID of the release
before it (null opens a Nexus's first) and the floor manifest's CID (`floor-release.ts`). Its canonical bytes name its
CID, which keys its row on the crossroads board (`$:/lar/floor-release/<cid>`); a reader RE-DERIVES that CID from the
body and refuses on a mismatch before it trusts anything else.

''TWO HANDS, KEPT APART.'' A seated Kahu key seals the body's bytes as the steward (the publisher, face-free). The
cabal's authority rides attached: a promotion approval over the manifest's EXACT BYTES under the surface
`floor-release`, which the chartered classes hold at the high-impact bar. A reader verifies the shape, the CID, the
Nexus, the epoch, the steward's seat and seal, the manifest bytes against their CID, then the approval at the
roster's threshold. A seal over any other shape (a cabal act, a pointer body) verifies as nothing here.

''THE CHAIN.'' A release counts when it verifies and either opens the chain (`prior` null) or links to a release that
counts. The head stands as the counted release no other counted release names; two heads read `unsettled`, and a face
accepts neither until the cabal settles the fork. `lares nexus release <manifest-file> --approval <file>` seals and
lands a release (`nexus-release.ts`); `lares nexus promote open|sign` carries the approval between vaults. A herm carries
the answer and signs nothing; a face accepts a release by a person's own act (`stable-founding-road` W26 slice 5).

''THE LINEAGE RIDES THE RECORD.'' The record the porch serves is `{body, cid, seal, approval, lineage}`. The lineage is the
charter's public epoch lineage from genesis through the release's epoch: the genesis epoch re-derives the Nexus AID,
each step verifies, and the last epoch's seats form the roster. A herm and a browser therefore verify a release with
no charter in hand (`floor-answer.ts`, `lararium-browser` `browser-floor-read.ts`). The flow map keeps its own pointer
under the `flow-pointer` domain.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/distinction>>

!! Kahu vs. cap=admin

`cap=admin` names a ''capability rung'' — what a given actor may do to a given bag.

//Kahu// names a ''role'' — who carries the curatorial responsibility for the living grammar
across the whole DreamNet, or the admission acts for one Nexus.

These two live in separate registers:

| Register | Term | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| ABILITY_LADDER | `cap=admin` | may touch lower bag layers; may MOVE tiddlers into corpus; may grant/revoke |
| Role / governance, DreamNet scale | corpus-kahu | curator of the living grammar — a lexicographer, never a legislator; seated from the Amorphous Dreams Cabal |
| Role / governance, Nexus scale | kahu quorum | admit · kapae · seal for ONE Nexus; k-of-n over that Nexus's seal lineage |
| Role / governance, Nexus scale | cadence threshold | offers ONE Nexus's engine cadence; a self-governing lineage seated apart from the kahu quorum, held by those who bear the reboot cost |

An operator may hold `cap=admin` on their own cabalGroup's infrastructure bags. That
does not make them kahu of DreamNet. Corpus-kahu attaches to the //corpus// specifically —
`lararium`, `lares`, and the genesis artifact — and a Nexus's kahu seat attaches to that
Nexus's seal lineage alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/cabal-surface>>

!! The corpus-kahu ~ seated from the Amorphous Dreams Cabal

The corpus-kahu hold kahu over DreamNet's protocol corpus. The Amorphous Dreams Cabal names the
community they come from; the kahu seat names a key-set and a threshold, never the Cabal's people. In
practice this means:

* They hold `cap=admin` on the `lararium` and `lares` Keyhive bag docs.
* Curated lifts (new sigil tiddlers, new protocol invariants, new ABILITY_LADDER rungs)
  land in the corpus bags as a record of use, never as dispatch.
* No vessel obeys a live command from the corpus-kahu. Vessels enforce their own founding
  proofs and capability gates.
* The corpus-kahu cannot reach inside an operator's vessel. Their admin grant on corpus bags
  carries zero authority over operator content, personGroup membership, or cabalGroup
  social structure.

''The grant binds the corpus-kahu, never crowns them.'' The refined steward
model (PROPOSED) draws that corpus `cap=admin` as a ''k-of-n threshold'' — the corpus-kahu
structurally cannot sign as one hand — over a grammar that stays ''forkable'' by
construction, so a drifting kahu gets forked away from rather than obeyed.
''Legitimacy flows from the BINDING, never from trust.'' The full four-part binding
(constitution-outside-reach · downward-accountable · k-of-n · auditable-and-forkable),
the steward-not-ruler doctrine, and the bounded break-glass / repair authority live at
<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/steward-doctrine">>.

The grammar lives in its speakers. The corpus-kahu keep its record. The vessels speak it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/neighboring-hawaiian-roles>>

!! Neighboring Roles

Hawaiian vocabulary places kahu in a lattice of related guardianship forms.
These may name adjacent roles as the DreamNet social layer develops:

* ''Kahuna'' — expert practitioner in a field; applied to specialists, scholars, priests.
  Maps toward domain operators who hold deep expertise in a particular kumu-device
  or wiki namespace. Does not carry protocol-layer authority.
* ''Kupuna'' — elder embodying aloha, pono, mālama; keeper of wisdom through lived
  practice. Maps toward long-standing operators whose vessels carry deep cabal history.
  Honorific, not a capability rung.
* ''Kumu'' — source, teacher, foundation. Already in use as the kumu-device interface
  (reactive event surface). No collision.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/ea-notes>>

!! Ea Notes

The Keyhive gates prove vessel identity and cabal affiliation; neither encodes kahu.
* Boot proves IDENTITY ONLY: the vessel holds a valid Individual keypair (Gate A), and a device
  delegation signed by its persona KEL's head key names this vessel's key (the Binding Gate).
* Gate C — the PersonaGroup's membership in a MeshCabal — stands off the boot path. A PersonaGroup
  belongs to many cabals or none, so membership reads as a held capability, checked at ACCESS to a
  cabal's shared canon (`boot-daemon-keyhive.ts`). For the Kahu cabal `kahuCabalCanonAccess` decides it (#/cabal-admission), and every write door into system canon calls it.

Gate C checks cabal MEMBERSHIP, never the quorum's seats. Who holds kahu over the corpus bags
rides a separate claim, encoded in the Keyhive docs for `lararium` and `lares`. The genesis
founding ceremony initializes those docs with the corpus-kahu's seated keys and threshold —
keys, never a membership list — and needs no subsequent runtime authority transfer.

<<~/ahu>>

